"Each GPS satellite carries several atomic clocks, which are extremely precise, and keeps broadcasting a simple message: which satellite it is, exactly where it is, and the exact time it sent the message. Your phone never sends anything back. It only listens. The signal is a radio wave, so it travels at the speed of light. From a satellite straight overhead, it takes about 0.07 seconds to reach you. Your phone compares the time in the message with the time the signal arrived, and travel time multiplied by the speed of light gives the distance to that satellite.": "2ca3c5f2d821",
"GPS stands for Global Positioning System. The United States military started the project in 1973 and launched the first satellite in 1978. At first, ordinary users got a signal that was blurred on purpose, so it was less accurate. On May 1, 2000, the United States turned the blurring off, and since then everyone gets the accurate signal for free. The system needs at least 24 satellites, and for more than ten years about 31 have been working. They fly about 20,200 kilometers up, and each one circles Earth twice a day.": "122d6ea61323",
"How does your phone turn a signal's travel time into a distance?": "38bd77ae2ae4",
"Make sure your answer is accurate before you hand it in.": "07c328a572e9",
"One distance is not enough. If you are 21,000 kilometers from one satellite, you could be anywhere on a huge circle on Earth's surface. A second satellite draws a second circle, and the two circles cross at only two points. A third satellite's circle passes through just one of them, and that point is you. Finding a place by measuring distances like this is called trilateration. It uses distances, not angles.": "9cc254d33856",
"Open a map app, and a blue dot shows where you are, even on a quiet farm road in Changhua. Sometimes a light blue circle surrounds the dot, and the smaller the circle, the surer the app is. Walk between tall buildings, and the dot may jump to the next street. Nobody typed in your address. So how does your phone know where you are? It listens to satellites more than 20,000 kilometers above your head.": "6489742c084a",
"Open a map app, and a blue dot shows where you are, even on a quiet farm road in Changhua. Sometimes a light blue circle surrounds the dot, and the smaller the circle, the surer the app is. Walk between tall buildings, and the dot may jump to the next street. Nobody typed in your address. So how does your phone know where you are? It listens to satellites more than 20,000 kilometers above your head. GPS stands for Global Positioning System. The United States military started the project in 1973 and launched the first satellite in 1978. At first, ordinary users got a signal that was blurred on purpose, so it was less accurate. On May 1, 2000, the United States turned the blurring off, and since then everyone gets the accurate signal for free. The system needs at least 24 satellites, and for more than ten years about 31 have been working. They fly about 20,200 kilometers up, and each one circles Earth twice a day. Each GPS satellite carries several atomic clocks, which are extremely precise, and keeps broadcasting a simple message: which satellite it is, exactly where it is, and the exact time it sent the message. Your phone never sends anything back. It only listens. The signal is a radio wave, so it travels at the speed of light. From a satellite straight overhead, it takes about 0.07 seconds to reach you. Your phone compares the time in the message with the time the signal arrived, and travel time multiplied by the speed of light gives the distance to that satellite. One distance is not enough. If you are 21,000 kilometers from one satellite, you could be anywhere on a huge circle on Earth's surface. A second satellite draws a second circle, and the two circles cross at only two points. A third satellite's circle passes through just one of them, and that point is you. Finding a place by measuring distances like this is called trilateration. It uses distances, not angles. There is a catch. Light travels about 300 meters in one millionth of a second, so a tiny clock error turns into a big mistake. Your phone has a cheap quartz clock, not an atomic one. The fix is a fourth satellite. With four distances, the phone can work out its position and its own clock error at the same time. That is why GPS needs at least four satellites. Even Einstein matters here: the satellites are high up, where gravity is weaker, and they move fast, so their clocks would gain about 38 microseconds a day. Light travels about 11 kilometers in that time, so engineers set the satellite clocks to tick a tiny bit slower before launch. Today your phone listens to more than GPS. Many phones also use Europe's Galileo, China's BeiDou, Russia's GLONASS, and Japan's QZSS, which serves the Asia-Oceania region. More satellites in view mean a better fix between tall buildings. Indoors, where satellite signals are weak, phones also use Wi-Fi and the cell towers from Lesson 6. Under open sky, a phone's GPS is usually accurate to about 5 meters. Near tall buildings, signals bounce off walls and travel farther, so the distances come out wrong and the blue dot drifts.": "42e9575267e8",
